WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:16.443
- Hi everyone.

00:00:16.443 --> 00:00:19.040
Welcome to our Daily Homeroom live stream.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.040
This is our way at Khan Academy

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.520
of keeping everyone in touch
during school closures.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.670
Obviously as an
organization with a mission

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:28.950
of providing a free world class education

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.030
for anyone, anywhere.

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:33.530
We put a huge emphasis on

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:35.060
how do we put our materials together

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:36.700
to support parents and teachers.

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.620
We've been running parent
webinars, teacher webinars

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:42.120
and this is really a part of that process.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.800
I do like to remind folks
we are not for profit.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:46.240
If you're in a position to do so,

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:48.840
please think about making
a donation to Khan Academy.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:53.040
We were running at a deficit
even before this crisis

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.090
and our deficit has
gotten a little bit worse

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:56.720
now that our costs have gone up.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:58.730
I do wanna give a special thanks to

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:01.631
several corporations
that have helped us out

00:01:01.631 --> 00:01:03.570
especially through this crisis.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:05.440
Bank of America, Google.org,

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.100
AT&amp;T, Novartis and Fastly.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.200
They stepped up in really record time

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.910
and even with their significant help,

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.050
we're still digging into our reserves

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:16.430
in order to be able to do the work we do.

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:18.590
So once again, if you're
in a position to do so,

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:20.990
please think about donating.

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:24.690
So I am super excited
about our guest today

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.150
and I see that I'm
freezing up a little bit,

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:27.290
at least on my screen.

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:28.160
Philippe in the background,

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:30.830
let me know if I'm freezing
up a little bit too much

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:33.360
but my guest today is Jim Nondorf,

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.180
who is the Head of Admissions
at University of Chicago.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:38.850
Is that a fair way of describing you, Jim?

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:42.580
And we're gonna be talking
about college admissions

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:44.570
and all the rest.

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:46.640
So I've been told, let
me close my other tabs

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:49.600
that might be slowing down my computer

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:55.100
but so maybe a good
place to start, Jim, is,

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:58.920
tell us how are you in college admissions

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.740
at University of Chicago but I'm assuming

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:02.580
it's indicative of a lot of other places.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.800
How are you thinking
about the COVID crisis

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:07.820
and next year's admissions?

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:10.210
- Oh, well we're thinking
about it in a lot of ways.

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:11.970
Obviously the university as a whole

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:15.080
is making plans for
what we can do on campus

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:16.880
and what we will do online.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:18.920
Currently our students are online

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:22.041
as almost every school that
I can think of is doing.

00:02:22.041 --> 00:02:24.700
In terms of the admissions efforts,

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:27.300
we are kinda doubling down and making sure

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.910
that all the things that a student needs

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:32.330
to do, to learn about our school,

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:35.550
to learn about how to apply
to highly selective schools,

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:40.160
to write good essays, to
showcase their academic promise,

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.290
that all of that is being
communicated to them

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.080
in a whole host of ways.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:48.570
We have opened up so many
different online tours,

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:51.643
info sessions, Spanish
information sessions.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:54.620
We've got alumni doing work,

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:56.763
we've got tour guide panels

00:02:56.763 --> 00:03:00.020
and all of this is not just
about the university of Chicago,

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:03.390
it's really to help all
the students understand,

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:07.160
what do you need to do over
the next six to seven months

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:09.800
to be prepared to apply to any school

00:03:11.068 --> 00:03:13.070
and in some odd way they're,

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:15.290
you can actually do more
and be better prepared

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:17.450
to put a great application together

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.310
through all the resources
that use coverage.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.310
I know my peers are putting together

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:24.090
for our students.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:26.550
So, this is not the time to panic.

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:30.070
This is actually the time to
focus on the things you can do,

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:31.393
not what you can't do.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:34.560
- And I wanna remind all all the viewers,

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:36.480
whether you're on YouTube or Facebook,

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:37.420
put your questions in.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:38.770
We have team members who are monitoring.

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:41.660
This is your chance to
ask Jim really anything

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:43.110
and I'm happy to answer any questions

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:44.750
that you might have as well.

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:46.320
But Jim, maybe a good place,

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:48.120
as you mentioned, this
isn't the time to panic,

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:49.450
everything's gonna be fine

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:50.900
but we've had previous sessions

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:52.950
where we talked about the SAT and AP tests

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:56.010
and kids were worried about
some tests getting canceled

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:58.110
or they're different this year.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:01.560
How are y'all thinking
about the test optionality

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:04.200
of things or these new types of tests?

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.746
- Sure, so testing and the fact that

00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:09.370
students have gone, are online

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:11.390
doing their own high school work

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:12.757
and some of their grading

00:04:12.757 --> 00:04:16.720
has gone from letter grades to pass-fail.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:18.000
All of this is happening

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.410
and I wanna assure all the families

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:24.210
that admissions officers do
not have their heads stuck

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:27.100
in the sand or stuck up other places,

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:29.056
which I've been told I do on occasion.

00:04:29.056 --> 00:04:31.970
We actually know what's
going on in the world

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:35.360
and we are trying to
accommodate those things.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.870
So, I'll start, I guess with the testing.

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:41.000
So many students have taken tests.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.120
For those students that
haven't taken tests,

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:46.110
there will be testing
available in the Fall.

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:48.470
There's also U.Chicago is test optional.

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:50.960
So we don't require testing.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:53.200
You can submit your testing
if you've already taken it

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:54.130
and you're good at it

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:56.710
or you could print out some
of the work you've done

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:59.070
on Khan and send that in and say,

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:02.801
and tell us, "Oh, I've
achieved mastery of this."

00:05:02.801 --> 00:05:04.530
There's all kinds of tests,

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:08.580
APs, SAT 2s, ACT all of this.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:10.970
If you've done it and you
wanna submit it, great.

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:14.140
Many schools are going to
be test optional this year.

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:17.640
So some of the flagship state publics,

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:20.330
many of my peers and there's
over a thousand schools

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:22.040
that were already test optional.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:25.340
For the schools that are still
going to require testing,

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:29.700
I guarantee there will be testing
opportunities in the Fall,

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:32.730
either live in person the way it had been.

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:37.620
I know the SAT, the College
Board is adding test sittings.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:40.180
And so every month, I
believe beginning in August,

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:41.970
there will be a test sitting

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:44.740
and then if no one is allowed to,

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:47.620
if, through the virus gets worse

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:50.050
and we're all back home

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:54.000
and unable to go to a test center

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.430
and take tests with other
people, the SAT will be online.

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:01.790
They have guaranteed that they
will offer an online version.

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:03.660
So whether you're applying to schools

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:06.930
that require testing or
do not require testing,

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:08.610
everything will be fine.

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:10.740
Just continue to study hard, work hard

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:12.670
and do the things you can do,

00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:14.790
everything will be...

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:16.410
There's no need to worry about it,

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:18.110
I guess is the best way to say it.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:21.140
- And we're getting a ton of questions

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:22.540
and I actually want to
get to these questions.

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:24.070
But you mentioned test optional

00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:25.450
and I think test optional has always been

00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:27.610
a little bit confusing for some students.

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:30.510
What's your read of test optional?

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:32.960
Does it mean if you do
well on the SAT or ACT,

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:33.793
that's great.

00:06:33.793 --> 00:06:35.203
What if you didn't do well,

00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:38.703
are you at a disadvantage somehow?

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:42.280
- You definitely are not at a disadvantage

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:45.700
applying without testing
to test optional schools.

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:47.140
So the way I think of testing is

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:50.550
it's one way of showcasing
your academic promise.

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:53.830
Some students, instead
of submitting testing,

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:56.936
they submit amazing artwork or music.

00:06:56.936 --> 00:06:59.383
I have students who have gotten,

00:07:00.547 --> 00:07:03.060
they, have built business plans,

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:05.050
they have patents already,

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:07.070
they run companies.

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:10.740
I have students who just
get a little nervous

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:11.710
about taking testing.

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:15.700
You have students who have
not had access to test prep.

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:19.330
You have students who have,
the worst of all sins,

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:21.250
they did not know about Khan Academy

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:23.470
and so these students,

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:27.310
testing didn't showcase their very best.

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:29.620
The testing does not showcase who they are

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:32.100
and so, send me something
else in lieu of that,

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:34.000
a paper that you're really proud of,

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:36.700
there's no reason to feel,

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:38.570
and every school will tell you this.

00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:40.560
Testing is not the be-all end-all.

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:43.050
It can be an important
part of your application,

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:44.433
but it doesn't have to be.

00:07:46.001 --> 00:07:48.270
- That's super helpful

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:50.530
and just going with
that theme from YouTube,

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:54.320
Ben QB asks, will you guys
look at AP tests from this year

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:56.110
'cause AP test is clearly
a little bit different.

00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:58.210
How are y'all thinking about that?

00:07:58.210 --> 00:07:59.043
- We will.

00:07:59.043 --> 00:08:01.980
So, we never required
the AP tests in the past

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:03.110
and we will look at them

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:05.225
essentially the same way
we've looked at them.

00:08:05.225 --> 00:08:07.900
There are students who have taken the AP

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:11.750
and decided to take the AP online tests,

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:15.116
we'll treat them as valid just
as we did any other testing.

00:08:15.116 --> 00:08:17.110
We have crediting policies

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:19.360
for students who were admitted and come

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:24.110
and so, no change in
how we will view the APs

00:08:24.110 --> 00:08:25.290
and I think that will be the case

00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:28.405
for most of my peers.

00:08:28.405 --> 00:08:31.430
Here again, if a student is nervous

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:33.840
about taking their testing online,

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:35.980
I'm sure we will understand,

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:39.120
okay, some students just won't
take the AP test this year

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:42.600
because they were nervous
about doing it online

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:43.757
and we will understand that

00:08:43.757 --> 00:08:46.053
and be accommodating for that.

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:48.500
- And I actually wanna
double click on that

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:49.610
'cause there is this perception,

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:51.780
especially with students who
might wanna go to a place

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:53.430
like university of Chicago,

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:55.390
they have these archetypes in their mind.

00:08:55.390 --> 00:08:58.620
The kid who gets a five on five AP tests

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:00.620
and then she's the,

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:02.770
gets a 1500 on their SAT

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:04.180
and they do X, Y, and Z.

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:06.060
But some of what you just said,

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:07.290
it isn't that.

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:08.870
You're saying there's other ways,

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:10.650
but I think there might be
people who are skeptical.

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:12.067
They're like, "Well, he's just saying that

00:09:12.067 --> 00:09:14.030
"'cause he wants my application."

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:15.920
Convince us.
(chuckling)

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:17.123
- No, all all right, sure, I will.

00:09:17.123 --> 00:09:19.190
All right, so I'll start with,

00:09:19.190 --> 00:09:20.850
so at places like Chicago,

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:22.122
I used to work for Yale,

00:09:22.122 --> 00:09:25.518
I go to all these meetings
with the other Ivy deans.

00:09:25.518 --> 00:09:28.580
At Chicago, I get about 300 kids

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:30.840
with perfect SATs apply every year

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:33.140
and we reject 60% of them.

00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:34.760
Now that's supposed to make you feel good.

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:36.570
If you're online, don't freak out.

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:38.080
This is supposed to make you feel good.

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:40.690
It basically says that the testing

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:43.170
is only one piece of holistic review

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:45.950
and we care about your
grades and your activities

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:47.080
and your essays.

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:49.410
In fact, the vast
majority of my applicants

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:50.650
are more than qualified.

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:52.950
I have about 35,000 applicants,

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:56.220
30,000 of them would do
really well at UChicago

00:09:56.220 --> 00:09:58.060
and when you get down to the last 10,000,

00:09:58.060 --> 00:10:01.380
essentially, academically,
they're identical.

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:03.850
Everybody has got good
grades, good testing,

00:10:03.850 --> 00:10:04.970
put things together

00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:06.856
and when you get down to the last 5,000,

00:10:06.856 --> 00:10:09.650
essentially I could kill
everyone in the freshmen class

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:10.900
and replace them with the wait list

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:11.890
and nobody would know the difference.

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:13.657
No, I'm kidding, I would never do that.

00:10:13.657 --> 00:10:15.180
- Jim, be careful,

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:16.188
people might--
(laughs softly)

00:10:16.188 --> 00:10:19.070
- We're all ready of
the applicants, is great

00:10:19.070 --> 00:10:21.020
and that's not what differentiates you.

00:10:21.020 --> 00:10:23.540
That's not what makes
you a UChicago student.

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:25.480
What makes you a UChicago student

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:27.080
or what you put into the essays

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:29.600
and how you respond and
the kind of person you are

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:30.740
in your love of learning

00:10:30.740 --> 00:10:33.250
and if that comes through
in the application,

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:34.223
then we admit you.

00:10:35.450 --> 00:10:36.317
- And, actually--

00:10:36.317 --> 00:10:38.110
- Don't ever kill somebody
Sal, that was a joke.

00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:41.270
- I know, you're one of the
nicest of people I know so,

00:10:41.270 --> 00:10:43.443
yeah Jim does not do any killing.

00:10:43.443 --> 00:10:45.580
One thing, you just touched on that,

00:10:45.580 --> 00:10:48.300
put in your essays, that authenticity,

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:50.040
that's something we talk a lot about.

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:52.020
I love your insider's point of view

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:54.220
of how you spot authentic versus

00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:56.354
inauthentic applications.

00:10:56.354 --> 00:10:59.090
- Sure, so one of the things you'll hear

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:01.970
from almost any of the
highly selective deans

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:04.690
is that everything kinda holds together.

00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:09.570
So the student's voice that
you hear in their first essay,

00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:12.560
which is about kind of who they are,

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:15.130
with the background, what they believe in,

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:19.020
that that voice will then
carry on in their second essay,

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:21.210
which is typically unique to each school.

00:11:21.210 --> 00:11:24.350
So we have our zany UChicago prompts

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:28.400
and the voice and the kind of
person you're meeting there

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:29.550
will be in the second essay,

00:11:29.550 --> 00:11:30.860
they'll be in the third one,

00:11:30.860 --> 00:11:33.200
which is, why do you
want to go to my school?

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:34.800
Why are you a good fit for you to Chicago?

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:38.360
And then if the voice is
consistent across all of those

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:40.080
and the kind of kid you're hearing,

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:43.630
the teacher recommendations
will reinforce that same thing

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:45.890
and so you can really tell

00:11:45.890 --> 00:11:48.570
when somebody else has been involved

00:11:48.570 --> 00:11:51.180
and so if I had one strong piece of advice

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:53.610
for all the families listening
and students listening,

00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:57.550
it's don't let adults get
involved in writing your essays.

00:11:57.550 --> 00:11:59.573
We can smell them a mile away.

00:11:59.573 --> 00:12:01.810
You should sound like a 17 year old,

00:12:01.810 --> 00:12:03.700
not a 41 year old lawyer.

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:06.340
And so, all the work,

00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:10.940
the whole application should
kind of tell the same story

00:12:10.940 --> 00:12:14.930
and a story of the kind of
kid you want on your campus.

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:16.490
Did that,

00:12:16.490 --> 00:12:18.034
make sense?
- That's super helpful.

00:12:18.034 --> 00:12:19.860
If you wanna share at any point,

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:21.875
I'd love to hear stories of applications

00:12:21.875 --> 00:12:23.510
that were clearly written

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:25.386
by the 41 year old lawyer versus...

00:12:25.386 --> 00:12:27.190
(laughs)
- Oh my God.

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:29.116
I had this one that I really just...

00:12:29.116 --> 00:12:32.430
So I have one where it
was from another school

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:34.650
but their Dean and I were talking

00:12:34.650 --> 00:12:36.967
and it was like, "I was
on their campus and,

00:12:36.967 --> 00:12:40.507
"the only thing buzzing more than the bees

00:12:40.507 --> 00:12:43.420
"was me at all the
academic opportunities,"

00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:48.250
and I was like, no 17 year old
would ever write like that.

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:49.770
But on the flip side,

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:52.950
I will never forget
this one wonderful essay

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:54.360
that I read from a girl

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:56.610
that I referred to as the cannibal essay,

00:12:56.610 --> 00:12:59.580
where she was at an all girls
Catholic school in California

00:12:59.580 --> 00:13:03.020
and our essay prompt that
year was cats and dogs,

00:13:03.020 --> 00:13:06.400
monteques and capulets,
auto bots and decepticons.

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:08.600
There are two kinds of people
in this world, what are they?

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:10.610
And she wrote about how
there was an earthquake

00:13:10.610 --> 00:13:11.700
and they were all trapped,

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:13.660
how she would divide the student body

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:15.570
into those students she would eat

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:17.090
and the students she would not eat

00:13:17.090 --> 00:13:18.820
and it was hysterical and funny

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:22.160
and something clearly only
a 17 year old would write

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:23.537
and the last part was,

00:13:23.537 --> 00:13:26.157
"And so, it's not whether
they're juicy and succulent,

00:13:26.157 --> 00:13:27.447
"it's whether they're interesting

00:13:27.447 --> 00:13:29.437
"and that's why I would eat them

00:13:29.437 --> 00:13:30.787
"and that's why I wanna go to UChicago

00:13:30.787 --> 00:13:33.524
"to be around interesting
people and eat them."

00:13:33.524 --> 00:13:36.320
We admitted her and I
checked her roommates

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:38.723
and they're always alive
so everything's fine.

00:13:38.723 --> 00:13:39.680
(mumbling)

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:42.713
It's memorable, it speaks to who they are.

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:45.210
- No, this has got a little bit morbid

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:46.270
on some of these points

00:13:46.270 --> 00:13:49.490
but I think your point
is exactly right that,

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:50.910
these things that are quirky,

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:53.100
that aren't a template,

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:55.210
that show clear creativity
definitely stand out

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:56.043
clearly for you.

00:13:56.043 --> 00:13:59.110
Did we make the Jim
Nondorfs of the world smile

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:00.640
and remember that,

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:03.070
seems like a pretty good indicator.

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:05.120
So a lot more questions here from YouTube.

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:06.470
Aditya Gupta asks,

00:14:06.470 --> 00:14:08.920
how has the inability to compete in sports

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:10.360
change in standardized testing

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:13.120
along with change in access
to extracurricular activities,

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:15.750
affecting the admissions process?

00:14:15.750 --> 00:14:17.510
- Oh, that's a great question

00:14:17.510 --> 00:14:20.320
because, well, it falls
into two categories.

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:22.400
So first there's like the athletes

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:24.330
who thought or are hoping to be recruited

00:14:24.330 --> 00:14:27.460
and you should not worry
because the coaches have,

00:14:27.460 --> 00:14:29.170
you're gonna be submitting testing.

00:14:29.170 --> 00:14:31.815
I mean, videos,

00:14:31.815 --> 00:14:33.950
some coaches may reach out to you

00:14:33.950 --> 00:14:37.420
and ask you to video yourself
doing certain activities,

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:39.620
whether it's how fast you run

00:14:39.620 --> 00:14:42.270
or agility drills and things like that.

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:46.500
The varsity coaches will
lead you along the way

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:48.300
and the process for getting that started

00:14:48.300 --> 00:14:50.150
is pretty much the same for everybody.

00:14:50.150 --> 00:14:52.600
You go online to the schools
you're interested in,

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:55.240
you go to the websites of
the athletic departments,

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:57.490
the specific team that you're interested

00:14:57.490 --> 00:14:58.360
and they will,

00:14:58.360 --> 00:15:00.807
there's a form for you
to fill out that says,

00:15:00.807 --> 00:15:01.750
"I'm interested,"

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:04.160
and they ask you all the details.

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:06.390
I can bench press this
much, run this fast,

00:15:06.390 --> 00:15:07.740
whatever the case might be.

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:11.963
So outside of being a recruited athlete,

00:15:12.830 --> 00:15:14.083
what you do,

00:15:15.720 --> 00:15:18.170
whatever you were doing,
we will take it that,

00:15:18.170 --> 00:15:19.647
we kinda look at it and say,

00:15:19.647 --> 00:15:21.537
"Well, they probably would have done that

00:15:21.537 --> 00:15:23.680
"this last spring,"

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:26.427
and whether you were
an athlete or a singer

00:15:26.427 --> 00:15:29.600
or you do community service,

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:32.380
you take care of an
elderly relative, you work,

00:15:32.380 --> 00:15:33.950
there's nothing wrong with working.

00:15:33.950 --> 00:15:36.370
Whether you're thespian, it doesn't...

00:15:36.370 --> 00:15:39.880
That what you specifically
do doesn't matter.

00:15:39.880 --> 00:15:42.190
What matters is that you're
passionate about something,

00:15:42.190 --> 00:15:44.360
that you were engaged
in it, that you like it,

00:15:44.360 --> 00:15:46.190
that you learn from it,

00:15:46.190 --> 00:15:47.580
that you learn teamwork

00:15:47.580 --> 00:15:49.840
and that can be done in many ways.

00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:52.440
And the fact that you had one season off

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:54.720
is not gonna make an impact at all.

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:56.969
Please do not worry about that.

00:15:56.969 --> 00:15:59.090
Every time I give talks,

00:15:59.090 --> 00:16:02.560
I was giving one right before
the stay at home orders

00:16:02.560 --> 00:16:05.410
with Stu Schmill who is a wonderful guy

00:16:05.410 --> 00:16:07.610
and one of my best friends at MIT.

00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:10.150
And he was saying how,

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:12.500
that we talk about this and we say,

00:16:12.500 --> 00:16:16.520
there is no set of things
that everybody has to check

00:16:16.520 --> 00:16:17.710
to get in.

00:16:17.710 --> 00:16:19.667
If you didn't do community
service, we're like,

00:16:19.667 --> 00:16:23.380
"Ooh, they must be killing
baby seals at night."

00:16:23.380 --> 00:16:24.250
We don't think that.

00:16:24.250 --> 00:16:27.096
It's not what you don't
do, it's what you do

00:16:27.096 --> 00:16:28.480
that we're interested in

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:32.171
and so really try not to worry about

00:16:32.171 --> 00:16:35.333
what you're missing, just
engage in something else.

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:38.900
- No, Jim, you're infectious.

00:16:38.900 --> 00:16:40.736
You make me wanna apply to college.

00:16:40.736 --> 00:16:41.569
(laughs)

00:16:41.569 --> 00:16:43.050
- Awesome, we would take you in a second.

00:16:43.050 --> 00:16:43.883
Now I know it.

00:16:45.530 --> 00:16:49.440
- So we're getting a whole
series of questions here

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:51.550
that are dealing with COVID from Facebook.

00:16:51.550 --> 00:16:54.310
Aditi Daga, do you think that trends

00:16:54.310 --> 00:16:57.110
for the class of '21 will
continue for future classes?

00:16:57.110 --> 00:16:59.500
What changes do you see for '22 onwards.

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:02.531
From Facebook, Carolyn Khan Sorkin asks,

00:17:02.531 --> 00:17:05.950
if many of the high
school class of '20 kids

00:17:05.950 --> 00:17:08.730
take a gap year, how will
this affect admissions,

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:12.003
I guess for wait list this
year and for next year's class?

00:17:13.940 --> 00:17:15.210
- Well, there are a lot of things there,

00:17:15.210 --> 00:17:16.043
so why don't I,

00:17:16.043 --> 00:17:17.988
I'll give you some of the...

00:17:17.988 --> 00:17:20.990
We're in unchartered
territory to be very honest.

00:17:20.990 --> 00:17:23.940
Who knows what's going to happen.

00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:26.070
I'll give you one of the
things that I am expecting.

00:17:26.070 --> 00:17:29.580
So right now, we don't have any more kids

00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:32.900
asking to take a gap
year than we've ever had.

00:17:32.900 --> 00:17:36.139
We always have about
15, 20 this time of year

00:17:36.139 --> 00:17:38.590
and they're doing really
cool and exciting things

00:17:38.590 --> 00:17:39.890
that I want them to do it.

00:17:40.762 --> 00:17:44.850
In terms of what things
are gonna take place.

00:17:44.850 --> 00:17:46.790
So here's my theory on this

00:17:46.790 --> 00:17:48.880
and I'd love to hear
your thoughts on, Sal.

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:51.970
So, maybe it's 15, 20 years ago,

00:17:51.970 --> 00:17:53.380
all of a sudden everyone realized

00:17:53.380 --> 00:17:54.670
it's a global world

00:17:54.670 --> 00:17:56.830
and we should have study abroad programs.

00:17:56.830 --> 00:17:58.240
Maybe it was longer ago

00:17:58.240 --> 00:18:02.470
but really everybody
jumped on to study abroad

00:18:02.470 --> 00:18:05.450
because you needed to know
that there are other cultures

00:18:05.450 --> 00:18:09.120
and engage them and it has
become an important part

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:13.440
of every school's academic
kind of a program.

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:15.360
And we are learning a lot

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:20.360
about what is useful in online teaching

00:18:20.550 --> 00:18:22.960
and I could imagine over the years ahead

00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:24.830
that there would be a reason

00:18:24.830 --> 00:18:28.740
to have online virtual classes

00:18:28.740 --> 00:18:31.980
even during your four years
with no other external pushes

00:18:31.980 --> 00:18:34.100
but because when you graduate,

00:18:34.100 --> 00:18:36.590
you are going to need to
know how to do these things,

00:18:36.590 --> 00:18:37.760
how to be self directed,

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:42.630
how to sit and listen to
a video for 45 minutes

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:44.560
and pull out the important things.

00:18:44.560 --> 00:18:46.600
It's what you're gonna do if
you work for Goldman Sachs,

00:18:46.600 --> 00:18:49.670
it's what you're gonna do
if you become a teacher,

00:18:49.670 --> 00:18:51.090
a lawyer, it doesn't matter.

00:18:51.090 --> 00:18:54.898
And so I think the skills

00:18:54.898 --> 00:18:58.950
that students will need in the future,

00:18:58.950 --> 00:19:01.950
that online education
could be a part of that,

00:19:01.950 --> 00:19:03.550
just like study abroad is.

00:19:03.550 --> 00:19:05.130
I don't think it's gonna be all online

00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:08.410
and I think you get enormous value

00:19:08.410 --> 00:19:11.530
in our residential four year model

00:19:11.530 --> 00:19:13.580
but I do think that
there's always something

00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:15.210
you can learn in every crisis.

00:19:15.210 --> 00:19:16.710
In every crisis, in every war,

00:19:16.710 --> 00:19:17.946
something comes up.

00:19:17.946 --> 00:19:20.190
We wouldn't have had penicillin

00:19:20.190 --> 00:19:21.340
if there weren't a war.

00:19:22.863 --> 00:19:26.200
These challenges create opportunities.

00:19:26.200 --> 00:19:30.450
I know I can say for my own
area, college admissions

00:19:30.450 --> 00:19:34.890
that we are going to continue
offering virtual activities,

00:19:34.890 --> 00:19:38.740
online activities, the
ability for students to...

00:19:38.740 --> 00:19:41.320
We're creating a jukebox,
essentially a jukebox.

00:19:41.320 --> 00:19:45.010
All of the videos of classes that we have

00:19:45.010 --> 00:19:47.632
and there will be more
opportunities for students

00:19:47.632 --> 00:19:50.950
because of this crisis
than there were in the past

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:53.440
and it will be more available to students

00:19:53.440 --> 00:19:56.110
of also socioeconomic backgrounds.

00:19:56.110 --> 00:19:59.440
It is great that a student can
actually get more information

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:03.000
about Chicago today than
they could've in the past

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:04.360
and all of it is free.

00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:06.610
They only basically have to go online

00:20:06.610 --> 00:20:09.778
as opposed to a hotel
room, a flight to Chicago

00:20:09.778 --> 00:20:10.970
and those things.

00:20:10.970 --> 00:20:12.550
So there's a lot of benefit

00:20:12.550 --> 00:20:13.910
that's going to come out of this.

00:20:13.910 --> 00:20:15.230
It's hard to see that now

00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:17.430
but I really do believe that.

00:20:17.430 --> 00:20:20.530
- And related to that, I know
your focus is on admissions

00:20:20.530 --> 00:20:22.660
but I'm sure you're a part
of the broader conversation

00:20:22.660 --> 00:20:23.600
at the university.

00:20:23.600 --> 00:20:26.610
How is the university thinking
about contingency planning

00:20:26.610 --> 00:20:28.330
for this back to school,

00:20:28.330 --> 00:20:30.173
or if the virus picks back up?

00:20:31.140 --> 00:20:34.520
- Sure, so every school
is thinking about this,

00:20:34.520 --> 00:20:35.520
I can tell you that.

00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:39.290
One of the huge values we have at Chicago

00:20:39.290 --> 00:20:41.080
is that we're also are part of

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:44.070
a multi-billion dollar hospital,

00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:45.720
the UChicago medical center

00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:48.190
and working with the epidemiologists,

00:20:48.190 --> 00:20:50.400
we are learning a lot about,

00:20:50.400 --> 00:20:55.400
how do you operate in the
midst of the epidemic?

00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:58.560
How do you make sure that
doctors don't get sick?

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:00.690
How do you make sure
nurses don't get sick?

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:03.330
All of the important
aspects of social distancing

00:21:03.330 --> 00:21:06.875
and using what kind of
masks and things like that

00:21:06.875 --> 00:21:11.140
and we're looking at how
do we take that expertise

00:21:11.140 --> 00:21:14.470
and that learning and
leverage it into the fall.

00:21:14.470 --> 00:21:16.930
So our first and highest goal

00:21:16.930 --> 00:21:19.600
will be to make sure that
our students are safe

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:20.790
and that they're back on campus

00:21:20.790 --> 00:21:22.726
learning the way we would want them to.

00:21:22.726 --> 00:21:26.220
Obviously you have
multiple contingency plans

00:21:26.220 --> 00:21:27.810
where maybe everybody doesn't come back

00:21:27.810 --> 00:21:30.800
or if things go terrible in the fall,

00:21:30.800 --> 00:21:33.760
I'm sure governors of many states

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:36.400
will tell their
universities they can't open

00:21:36.400 --> 00:21:38.370
but we are going to do everything we can

00:21:38.370 --> 00:21:39.860
to be open and to be teaching

00:21:39.860 --> 00:21:41.360
and engaging our students

00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:42.570
because we don't want that opportunity...

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:43.470
This is important.

00:21:43.470 --> 00:21:44.960
It's important for their education,

00:21:44.960 --> 00:21:47.166
it's important for the country

00:21:47.166 --> 00:21:48.850
and quite honestly,

00:21:48.850 --> 00:21:50.890
this is what the university is all about.

00:21:50.890 --> 00:21:53.490
I feel we're very well prepared for it,

00:21:53.490 --> 00:21:55.280
thankfully because we
have a great hospital

00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:56.260
right next door to us

00:21:56.260 --> 00:21:59.030
in a lot of places, they
don't have those experts.

00:21:59.030 --> 00:22:00.650
They don't have hospitals,

00:22:00.650 --> 00:22:01.820
essentially on their campus.

00:22:01.820 --> 00:22:04.730
Even places that have wonderful hospitals,

00:22:04.730 --> 00:22:05.563
they're not attached.

00:22:05.563 --> 00:22:07.080
They're in a different part of the city,

00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:08.160
they're on the other side

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:10.170
or they're not even in the same city.

00:22:10.170 --> 00:22:11.960
At Chicago, the medical center

00:22:11.960 --> 00:22:15.040
is essentially the next
quad over from the main quad

00:22:15.040 --> 00:22:18.550
and so we are committed
to engaging our students.

00:22:18.550 --> 00:22:20.110
We're gonna do a lot of virtual things

00:22:20.110 --> 00:22:22.140
online with them this summer.

00:22:22.140 --> 00:22:24.060
I'm going to do a lot
of virtual things online

00:22:24.060 --> 00:22:25.500
with prospects this summer

00:22:25.500 --> 00:22:27.850
and my hope is that we will have tours

00:22:27.850 --> 00:22:29.923
and our students will be back in the fall.

00:22:31.272 --> 00:22:32.105
- No, that's great.

00:22:32.105 --> 00:22:33.460
And obviously none of us really knows

00:22:33.460 --> 00:22:34.950
what's gonna happen but it sounds like

00:22:34.950 --> 00:22:37.290
y'all have a good handle on it.

00:22:37.290 --> 00:22:38.690
There's another question
about test optional

00:22:38.690 --> 00:22:39.820
and I'll extend a little bit.

00:22:39.820 --> 00:22:41.540
From YouTube, Golden Bucket asks,

00:22:41.540 --> 00:22:43.000
where's the list that has...

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:44.580
Oh no, actually there's a different one.

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:46.160
This is from Amy S.Morgenstein.

00:22:46.160 --> 00:22:48.040
That's the question I
wanted to ask from Facebook.

00:22:48.040 --> 00:22:50.320
Jim, what has been your
experience going test optional?

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:52.630
Do you ever miss the SAT as a tool?

00:22:52.630 --> 00:22:53.890
And I'll extend it a little bit

00:22:53.890 --> 00:22:56.230
because not only are the test optional,

00:22:56.230 --> 00:22:57.930
a lot of school districts I know

00:22:57.930 --> 00:23:01.370
went to pass-fail this past term,

00:23:01.370 --> 00:23:02.203
who knows what happened.

00:23:02.203 --> 00:23:04.020
So there's this general
trend that at least

00:23:04.020 --> 00:23:06.630
on these academic numerical
objective measures,

00:23:06.630 --> 00:23:09.550
you might have less data
than you typically have.

00:23:09.550 --> 00:23:10.640
Is that true,

00:23:10.640 --> 00:23:13.730
especially with test optional
and how do you navigate that?

00:23:13.730 --> 00:23:16.910
- Sure, so I guess I'd start with,

00:23:16.910 --> 00:23:21.910
you need to have some
kind of valid information

00:23:21.910 --> 00:23:24.380
that comes in and the
transcript is great for that,

00:23:24.380 --> 00:23:26.630
testing is great for that.

00:23:26.630 --> 00:23:29.000
What I think test optional schools do

00:23:29.000 --> 00:23:31.710
is we offer other opportunities.

00:23:31.710 --> 00:23:33.350
So there are other ways and other things

00:23:33.350 --> 00:23:36.090
that students can send
in, in terms of what do I,

00:23:36.090 --> 00:23:37.010
do I miss it?

00:23:37.010 --> 00:23:40.980
But to be honest, 85, 90% of my applicants

00:23:40.980 --> 00:23:42.730
do send in testing

00:23:42.730 --> 00:23:46.478
because they think testing
is a good representation

00:23:46.478 --> 00:23:49.020
of their academic quality,

00:23:49.020 --> 00:23:50.810
that's great.

00:23:50.810 --> 00:23:54.600
In terms of their worries about

00:23:54.600 --> 00:23:56.204
what else could they send in,

00:23:56.204 --> 00:23:58.790
I think a good applicant
and a good application

00:23:58.790 --> 00:24:00.500
and a good college search,

00:24:00.500 --> 00:24:02.580
all start with the same thing,

00:24:02.580 --> 00:24:06.070
which is, let me think about myself.

00:24:06.070 --> 00:24:08.500
What makes me unique,
what makes me special,

00:24:08.500 --> 00:24:10.740
what makes me a good fit for schools?

00:24:10.740 --> 00:24:12.320
Then you go and you visit schools

00:24:12.320 --> 00:24:14.860
and you learn about what makes them unique

00:24:14.860 --> 00:24:16.510
and eventually I'm a big believer,

00:24:16.510 --> 00:24:18.470
you'll get a vibe and
you can get that vibe,

00:24:18.470 --> 00:24:20.660
even electronically
and you'll get a sense,

00:24:20.660 --> 00:24:22.220
this is the right school for me.

00:24:22.220 --> 00:24:24.980
And then you look at
what they're looking for

00:24:24.980 --> 00:24:28.100
and you try to showcase those attributes

00:24:28.100 --> 00:24:29.540
in your application.

00:24:29.540 --> 00:24:31.090
I want to get really specific about this

00:24:31.090 --> 00:24:32.480
on the testing piece.

00:24:32.480 --> 00:24:36.370
So students, I sometimes
see students take the test

00:24:36.370 --> 00:24:39.490
three, five, seven, nine times

00:24:39.490 --> 00:24:41.000
and I am like, that's,

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:43.360
one, you look neurotic and two,

00:24:43.360 --> 00:24:46.760
you should write it down as
a extracurricular activity.

00:24:46.760 --> 00:24:48.620
You've taken it so many times.

00:24:48.620 --> 00:24:50.640
Testing is not to be
taken that many times,

00:24:50.640 --> 00:24:52.570
you take it a couple of times

00:24:52.570 --> 00:24:54.390
and that's what your score is

00:24:54.390 --> 00:24:56.929
and you can submit it if you'd like to.

00:24:56.929 --> 00:25:01.280
The reason I say that is
because within a hundred points,

00:25:01.280 --> 00:25:04.530
it just doesn't matter in the
admissions committee room.

00:25:04.530 --> 00:25:07.130
I see students, a student
who's taken the test,

00:25:07.130 --> 00:25:09.793
got a 1580 and then they
take it four more times.

00:25:10.750 --> 00:25:11.860
One, it's a waste of time

00:25:11.860 --> 00:25:15.294
and it doesn't reflect
positively on them as a person.

00:25:15.294 --> 00:25:17.520
And so it really is,

00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:19.020
the same thing with GPA.

00:25:19.020 --> 00:25:21.150
Like the most worthless
piece of information

00:25:21.150 --> 00:25:22.920
I get is the GPA because

00:25:22.920 --> 00:25:25.140
I get it in every conceivable format,

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:27.900
4.0, 5.0, 6.0, 10.0, 12.0,

00:25:27.900 --> 00:25:28.733
weighted, unweighted,

00:25:28.733 --> 00:25:30.040
dasas, quintas, quartas.

00:25:30.040 --> 00:25:32.490
Sometimes they just give
you smiley faces at schools

00:25:32.490 --> 00:25:34.870
and we still have to figure out a way

00:25:34.870 --> 00:25:36.977
to look at that applicant and say,

00:25:36.977 --> 00:25:39.830
"How did you do, what did you take,

00:25:39.830 --> 00:25:42.040
"have you prepared yourself?"

00:25:42.040 --> 00:25:44.590
And in general, most of my applicants

00:25:44.590 --> 00:25:48.940
have done very well on
their academic pieces.

00:25:48.940 --> 00:25:50.700
What's gonna make them stand out

00:25:50.700 --> 00:25:53.670
in an applicant pool like we have

00:25:53.670 --> 00:25:56.270
and like my very highly
selective peers have

00:25:56.270 --> 00:25:59.980
is what makes the rest of
their application compelling

00:25:59.980 --> 00:26:01.180
and that, quite honestly

00:26:01.180 --> 00:26:03.010
comes through teacher recommendations,

00:26:03.010 --> 00:26:05.130
the extracurriculars they do

00:26:05.130 --> 00:26:07.470
and in many ways their essays.

00:26:07.470 --> 00:26:10.720
These essays tell us so
much about their dreams

00:26:10.720 --> 00:26:12.720
and the kinds of students they are

00:26:12.720 --> 00:26:14.640
and the kind of learners they are

00:26:14.640 --> 00:26:17.040
and what kind of an impact
they're gonna make on our campus

00:26:17.040 --> 00:26:18.253
and on the world.

00:26:19.160 --> 00:26:22.320
- And, we talked about the
kids who do submit the test.

00:26:22.320 --> 00:26:23.300
There's that, as you mentioned,

00:26:23.300 --> 00:26:26.070
10 to 15% who for whatever
reasons might not.

00:26:26.070 --> 00:26:27.250
One, how do you view that

00:26:27.250 --> 00:26:30.820
and how are they able to show
at least on the academic side

00:26:30.820 --> 00:26:35.140
that they can be in the
same academic environment

00:26:35.140 --> 00:26:38.730
as those students who are
scoring very well on these tests.

00:26:38.730 --> 00:26:42.270
- Sure, so some of them
will actually take,

00:26:42.270 --> 00:26:44.143
spend a lot of time on Khan Academy

00:26:44.143 --> 00:26:48.150
and I can see that they
have acquired the skills.

00:26:48.150 --> 00:26:51.770
That's another excellent
way to validate your grades

00:26:51.770 --> 00:26:55.800
in high school is by doing
work on Khan Academy.

00:26:55.800 --> 00:26:59.630
That's like, not an easy one,
one that shows me two things,

00:26:59.630 --> 00:27:02.000
one, mastery but even more important,

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:06.200
it shows me work ethic, persistence.

00:27:06.200 --> 00:27:09.000
The kids who are not
naturally good at math

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:12.030
but keep plugging away at it,
that's a great skill to have.

00:27:12.030 --> 00:27:13.654
So that's one set.

00:27:13.654 --> 00:27:16.090
I would also say there's
another set of students

00:27:16.090 --> 00:27:18.820
for whom both the full test score

00:27:18.820 --> 00:27:21.960
is not as relevant to what
they're planning to do

00:27:21.960 --> 00:27:25.230
when they get to UChicago or many schools.

00:27:25.230 --> 00:27:27.600
Great example, art history.

00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:29.370
I can have an applicant who puts together

00:27:29.370 --> 00:27:32.110
this amazing art history application

00:27:32.110 --> 00:27:33.610
and they've done all of this work

00:27:33.610 --> 00:27:35.114
and they write these amazing essays

00:27:35.114 --> 00:27:38.610
and you can see they are
gonna get a PhD in art history

00:27:38.610 --> 00:27:42.030
and probably change a field

00:27:42.030 --> 00:27:45.110
and the fact that they have a 600 maybe,

00:27:45.110 --> 00:27:48.763
on their math SAT section,

00:27:49.710 --> 00:27:51.850
it doesn't matter to me.

00:27:51.850 --> 00:27:54.697
I don't need to know and it tells me that,

00:27:54.697 --> 00:27:57.430
what I need to know is can they do

00:27:57.430 --> 00:27:59.370
the match requirement at UChicago.

00:27:59.370 --> 00:28:01.980
I can look at their
transcript and it says,

00:28:01.980 --> 00:28:04.870
they have a B+ in AP Calculus.

00:28:04.870 --> 00:28:08.393
Sold, you are gonna be a
great UChicago art historian.

00:28:09.630 --> 00:28:10.510
- That's super useful

00:28:10.510 --> 00:28:12.061
and I guess related to that from Facebook,

00:28:12.061 --> 00:28:14.130
I have this question from Maroom Hammoud

00:28:14.130 --> 00:28:17.150
who asks, how to guide our kids
to the most suitable college

00:28:17.150 --> 00:28:18.680
or path in their higher education

00:28:18.680 --> 00:28:20.170
and I'll just add to that,

00:28:20.170 --> 00:28:22.430
there tends to sometimes be an obsession

00:28:22.430 --> 00:28:23.930
with the UChicago's of the world,

00:28:23.930 --> 00:28:26.670
the highly selective
universities, it causes anxiety.

00:28:26.670 --> 00:28:27.800
People try to,...

00:28:27.800 --> 00:28:29.160
Their whole ego, their identity

00:28:29.160 --> 00:28:30.460
is defined by where they go to school.

00:28:30.460 --> 00:28:33.250
What advice do you have for folks?

00:28:33.250 --> 00:28:34.760
- Well, so I'll start with,

00:28:34.760 --> 00:28:36.830
there is no be-all end-all school

00:28:36.830 --> 00:28:39.055
other than the University
of Chicago of course

00:28:39.055 --> 00:28:41.830
and in many ways,

00:28:41.830 --> 00:28:45.470
please do not equate
your sense of self worth,

00:28:45.470 --> 00:28:47.700
your value, your accomplishments

00:28:47.700 --> 00:28:50.520
to getting into any
highly selective school.

00:28:50.520 --> 00:28:52.510
That's not how it works.

00:28:52.510 --> 00:28:55.330
Our process is not about
evaluating students

00:28:55.330 --> 00:28:56.673
and saying, "This student is good

00:28:56.673 --> 00:28:58.310
"and this student is bad."

00:28:58.310 --> 00:29:00.370
This is an economics problem set.

00:29:00.370 --> 00:29:03.680
I have a limited number
of spaces for students

00:29:03.680 --> 00:29:06.961
and I have many more
wonderful, amazing students

00:29:06.961 --> 00:29:09.490
that I would love to take
than I can actually take

00:29:09.490 --> 00:29:10.860
and have on campus.

00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:13.440
I want you to know that
admissions officers

00:29:13.440 --> 00:29:16.330
become very attached to their applicants.

00:29:16.330 --> 00:29:18.950
Sometimes, people cry in
admissions committees,

00:29:18.950 --> 00:29:20.830
not me but sometimes they cry

00:29:20.830 --> 00:29:24.200
because they become so
attached to these students.

00:29:24.200 --> 00:29:26.780
And so, that's the first thing,

00:29:26.780 --> 00:29:30.460
this process is not about placing a value

00:29:30.460 --> 00:29:34.230
on you as a person or
on your accomplishments.

00:29:34.230 --> 00:29:35.380
The second piece of that

00:29:35.380 --> 00:29:37.300
is how do you go about thinking,

00:29:37.300 --> 00:29:39.740
where am I a good fit?

00:29:39.740 --> 00:29:42.635
And you are in a great position right now

00:29:42.635 --> 00:29:45.070
because every school is putting together

00:29:45.070 --> 00:29:49.100
a lot of ways for you to
do that from your home.

00:29:49.100 --> 00:29:51.480
If it were me, I would
start at a high level

00:29:51.480 --> 00:29:53.300
and say, okay, I'm gonna look at

00:29:53.300 --> 00:29:56.730
three large private research universities,

00:29:56.730 --> 00:29:59.830
websites and videos and tours
and attend a class online.

00:29:59.830 --> 00:30:03.080
And then I'm gonna do that for
three liberal arts colleges

00:30:03.080 --> 00:30:05.230
and I'm gonna do it for
three large public schools

00:30:05.230 --> 00:30:06.570
and I'm gonna get a sense

00:30:06.570 --> 00:30:09.470
of what the vibe of which ones I liked,

00:30:09.470 --> 00:30:11.450
what resonated with me.

00:30:11.450 --> 00:30:12.760
And after you do that,

00:30:12.760 --> 00:30:16.170
then you can go and look
deeper into that category.

00:30:16.170 --> 00:30:19.480
So let's say you looked at, UChicago

00:30:19.480 --> 00:30:23.360
into a much less positive
and totally inferior schools

00:30:23.360 --> 00:30:25.120
like Harvard and Princeton.

00:30:25.120 --> 00:30:27.870
And then you visit the
three of our schools

00:30:27.870 --> 00:30:31.220
and you say, "Wow, I really
like research universities."

00:30:31.220 --> 00:30:33.300
Well, then you can look
at other fine institutions

00:30:33.300 --> 00:30:35.850
like Stanford and Yale and everybody else

00:30:35.850 --> 00:30:39.102
but it's getting a sense of
what's a good fit for you

00:30:39.102 --> 00:30:40.410
at the beginning.

00:30:40.410 --> 00:30:42.040
And then once you've done that,

00:30:42.040 --> 00:30:44.603
that's when you start
the process of saying,

00:30:45.860 --> 00:30:47.580
why would they want me?

00:30:47.580 --> 00:30:49.860
Why am I a good fit for them?

00:30:49.860 --> 00:30:53.480
And it takes a little self
evaluation and examination

00:30:53.480 --> 00:30:58.480
and then you choose a nice,
balanced college list,

00:31:00.220 --> 00:31:03.370
which has some schools
that are highly selective

00:31:03.370 --> 00:31:04.530
and some that are selective

00:31:04.530 --> 00:31:07.760
and some that are less selective than that

00:31:07.760 --> 00:31:08.960
and some schools where you think

00:31:08.960 --> 00:31:11.920
you're an excellent fit
and you apply to them

00:31:11.920 --> 00:31:14.680
and I can tell you that in general,

00:31:14.680 --> 00:31:16.811
everybody pretty much goes to college.

00:31:16.811 --> 00:31:19.220
If you do these things,

00:31:19.220 --> 00:31:20.410
you will have options

00:31:20.410 --> 00:31:22.120
and that's what this process,

00:31:22.120 --> 00:31:24.773
a good process will
leave you with options.

00:31:25.801 --> 00:31:26.940
- It's super valuable.

00:31:26.940 --> 00:31:28.240
I have this question from YouTube.

00:31:28.240 --> 00:31:30.980
Aditya Gupta asks, Jim,
what happens if you come

00:31:30.980 --> 00:31:33.390
from a disadvantaged school
with limited resources

00:31:33.390 --> 00:31:36.270
compared to more schools
in affluent communities?

00:31:36.270 --> 00:31:39.260
How do you stand out
with limited resources?

00:31:39.260 --> 00:31:41.970
- Awesome, so there are many ways to do it

00:31:41.970 --> 00:31:43.100
and they're all the same ways

00:31:43.100 --> 00:31:45.940
that the well-resourced students can do.

00:31:45.940 --> 00:31:47.500
Anybody can write a great essay,

00:31:47.500 --> 00:31:51.023
anybody can engage locally
in their community.

00:31:51.900 --> 00:31:53.370
What anybody can,

00:31:53.370 --> 00:31:56.760
hopefully, if you don't have
internet access at home,

00:31:56.760 --> 00:31:58.510
eventually, libraries will open

00:31:58.510 --> 00:31:59.650
and you can go to the library,

00:31:59.650 --> 00:32:01.470
your local library and learn a little bit

00:32:01.470 --> 00:32:03.330
about these schools.

00:32:03.330 --> 00:32:04.620
Many schools...

00:32:04.620 --> 00:32:06.760
So we have a whole set of programs

00:32:06.760 --> 00:32:08.220
for first generation students.

00:32:08.220 --> 00:32:09.680
We have our Odyssey Program,

00:32:09.680 --> 00:32:11.650
which is essentially for families

00:32:11.650 --> 00:32:16.480
who have less financial means.

00:32:16.480 --> 00:32:20.496
We have Fly-Ins, we do
programming for these students

00:32:20.496 --> 00:32:23.340
with their community based organizations.

00:32:23.340 --> 00:32:25.930
We partner up with
programs like QuestBridge

00:32:25.930 --> 00:32:28.360
or Jack Kent Cooke Foundation

00:32:28.360 --> 00:32:32.070
and these programs where we essentially,

00:32:32.070 --> 00:32:34.250
if your school wants my admissions officer

00:32:34.250 --> 00:32:38.020
to come and give a talk to you alone

00:32:38.020 --> 00:32:40.370
or all of your classmates,

00:32:40.370 --> 00:32:42.080
we have those options available

00:32:42.080 --> 00:32:44.910
and so we are committed

00:32:44.910 --> 00:32:46.500
and we are not the only school.

00:32:46.500 --> 00:32:49.200
My peers are equally
committed to doing this

00:32:49.200 --> 00:32:52.360
and then we set aside hundreds
of millions of dollars

00:32:52.360 --> 00:32:55.459
to ensure that you can come
to the University of Chicago

00:32:55.459 --> 00:32:57.993
or Yale or Harvard or Stanford.

00:33:00.750 --> 00:33:05.040
We're a no-loan school,
so you can decide to come

00:33:05.040 --> 00:33:06.900
and there's no loans in your packages,

00:33:06.900 --> 00:33:08.511
you graduate without debt.

00:33:08.511 --> 00:33:12.870
The average, for students who are on PEL,

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:14.480
essentially everything is covered.

00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:17.450
If your family makes less than $60,000,

00:33:17.450 --> 00:33:19.070
it's free to go to UChicago

00:33:19.070 --> 00:33:22.160
and some of my peers have
similar programs like that

00:33:22.160 --> 00:33:24.780
and so I would say to you,

00:33:24.780 --> 00:33:26.900
don't let your current situation...

00:33:26.900 --> 00:33:28.640
So I was a first gen, low income student

00:33:28.640 --> 00:33:32.620
from Hammond, Indiana
and I went off to Yale

00:33:32.620 --> 00:33:34.555
and that was because...

00:33:34.555 --> 00:33:36.850
You make the most of what you're given

00:33:36.850 --> 00:33:39.370
and quite honestly, that stands out.

00:33:39.370 --> 00:33:43.040
What you do with the resources you have

00:33:43.040 --> 00:33:46.043
is what is what will make an
impact in your application.

00:33:47.030 --> 00:33:49.480
- I doubly wanna, triply wanna
underline what you just said

00:33:49.480 --> 00:33:52.510
'cause I think we probably
had very similar childhoods.

00:33:52.510 --> 00:33:56.380
Growing up, MIT was always the school that

00:33:56.380 --> 00:33:58.090
I didn't even know much about

00:33:58.090 --> 00:33:59.750
but somehow I wanted to go there

00:33:59.750 --> 00:34:01.910
but I used to look at the MSRP tuition

00:34:01.910 --> 00:34:03.810
and it's like my mother
made 16,000 a year.

00:34:03.810 --> 00:34:05.710
She was a cashier at a convenience store,

00:34:05.710 --> 00:34:07.410
I was on free lunch.

00:34:07.410 --> 00:34:09.410
I was like, I'm not
gonna be able to go there

00:34:09.410 --> 00:34:12.620
and it was my sister who
told me about financial aid.

00:34:12.620 --> 00:34:14.116
And so to your point,

00:34:14.116 --> 00:34:17.060
if you're, below 60,000,

00:34:17.060 --> 00:34:18.250
not even where my mom was

00:34:18.250 --> 00:34:20.750
but if your family doesn't make a lot,

00:34:20.750 --> 00:34:23.880
you can leave University of Chicago

00:34:23.880 --> 00:34:25.970
without, it sounds like with no debt,

00:34:25.970 --> 00:34:28.780
which is better than the
deal I had back in the 90s.

00:34:28.780 --> 00:34:31.320
- Oh yeah, financial
aid has improved a lot

00:34:31.320 --> 00:34:32.570
since you and I were in college,

00:34:32.570 --> 00:34:33.963
I have to tell you, Sal.

00:34:35.326 --> 00:34:37.710
- And how is the COVID
crisis changing things?

00:34:37.710 --> 00:34:38.950
We're seeing record unemployment,

00:34:38.950 --> 00:34:40.200
there's gonna be a lot more folks

00:34:40.200 --> 00:34:42.710
in that category unfortunately.

00:34:42.710 --> 00:34:44.500
I'm guessing the endowments are down

00:34:44.500 --> 00:34:45.680
because of the stock market.

00:34:45.680 --> 00:34:48.143
Is that gonna change, financial aid?

00:34:48.978 --> 00:34:51.220
- Well, what's been wonderful is that

00:34:52.600 --> 00:34:54.420
at the top schools,

00:34:54.420 --> 00:34:56.680
there has been a constant reaffirmation,

00:34:56.680 --> 00:34:59.040
certainly there has been at
the University of Chicago

00:34:59.040 --> 00:35:01.160
that the financial aid

00:35:01.160 --> 00:35:03.410
and supporting our low income students

00:35:03.410 --> 00:35:05.620
remains, we're committed to that

00:35:05.620 --> 00:35:07.600
and that is not changing,

00:35:07.600 --> 00:35:09.130
regardless of how things are going

00:35:09.130 --> 00:35:12.600
in the economy and alumni

00:35:12.600 --> 00:35:14.480
and our board have stepped up.

00:35:14.480 --> 00:35:15.610
There've been major campaigns,

00:35:15.610 --> 00:35:17.360
especially in the last year,

00:35:17.360 --> 00:35:19.160
giving to our Odyssey scholarships,

00:35:19.160 --> 00:35:22.533
which is the financial aid
for our low income students.

00:35:23.480 --> 00:35:25.730
Millions of dollars have
actually been raised

00:35:25.730 --> 00:35:30.730
since March to assist our
low income students in,

00:35:33.080 --> 00:35:36.360
the current students in
transitioning back to their homes,

00:35:36.360 --> 00:35:39.257
setting them up computers, wifi,

00:35:39.257 --> 00:35:42.724
paying for their trips home.

00:35:42.724 --> 00:35:45.400
That has been a major commitment

00:35:45.400 --> 00:35:49.920
and I don't see the very
top schools changing that.

00:35:49.920 --> 00:35:51.670
Thankfully, the government is also now

00:35:51.670 --> 00:35:53.740
kicking in some additional
funding for that

00:35:53.740 --> 00:35:55.050
through the Kira's Act.

00:35:55.050 --> 00:35:56.970
I would imagine there will be more of that

00:35:56.970 --> 00:36:00.030
if the crisis continues or worsens

00:36:00.030 --> 00:36:01.150
but the bottom line is,

00:36:01.150 --> 00:36:05.860
this is a fundamental piece
of who a place like Chicago is

00:36:05.860 --> 00:36:08.660
and some of our other
highly selective peers,

00:36:08.660 --> 00:36:12.680
which is, we are here
to bring the very best

00:36:12.680 --> 00:36:16.100
and brightest regardless of
socioeconomic background,

00:36:16.100 --> 00:36:17.910
regardless of cultural background,

00:36:17.910 --> 00:36:22.580
regardless of political
views and academic views.

00:36:22.580 --> 00:36:24.080
We want the best and brightest

00:36:24.080 --> 00:36:26.110
and you only get that

00:36:26.110 --> 00:36:29.300
if you're willing to put your finances

00:36:29.300 --> 00:36:31.280
behind that commitment.

00:36:31.280 --> 00:36:32.330
- That's super valuable.

00:36:32.330 --> 00:36:33.920
And we're getting a ton of questions.

00:36:33.920 --> 00:36:35.940
Unfortunately, we're running out of time.

00:36:35.940 --> 00:36:38.770
Jim, I would love to have you on again.

00:36:38.770 --> 00:36:39.920
Your energy is infectious

00:36:39.920 --> 00:36:42.280
and I think, clearly people are engaged.

00:36:42.280 --> 00:36:44.620
Maybe just a good end question.

00:36:44.620 --> 00:36:46.563
Pierce Schumann from YouTube says,

00:36:47.430 --> 00:36:48.660
what is the most impressive thing

00:36:48.660 --> 00:36:50.630
that you can see on someone's application?

00:36:50.630 --> 00:36:51.463
And I'll just extend it,

00:36:51.463 --> 00:36:52.430
what advice do you have?

00:36:52.430 --> 00:36:54.110
Someone's applying to you, to UChicago.

00:36:54.110 --> 00:36:56.800
They wanna be noticed by Jim Nondorf,

00:36:56.800 --> 00:36:58.563
what advice, what should
they do in their life

00:36:58.563 --> 00:37:00.588
and what should they do
on their application?

00:37:00.588 --> 00:37:03.600
- So the first and most important thing

00:37:03.600 --> 00:37:06.861
is don't ask what I think
you should be doing.

00:37:06.861 --> 00:37:09.620
You should think about who you are

00:37:09.620 --> 00:37:10.940
and be true to yourself.

00:37:10.940 --> 00:37:13.840
The best applications are authentic

00:37:13.840 --> 00:37:17.150
and really, it's like this is who I am

00:37:17.150 --> 00:37:20.480
and I like who I am and
you're gonna like who I am

00:37:20.480 --> 00:37:22.730
and I'm gonna show it to
you in your application.

00:37:22.730 --> 00:37:24.810
Those are the applications you remember

00:37:24.810 --> 00:37:26.170
and that stand out.

00:37:26.170 --> 00:37:29.197
The ones that, where a student is like,

00:37:29.197 --> 00:37:30.870
"Oh, I'll bet they'll wanna hear this,"

00:37:30.870 --> 00:37:34.017
or, "Oh, I should go do this activity

00:37:34.017 --> 00:37:36.330
"because it'll look
good on my application."

00:37:36.330 --> 00:37:39.320
That's not the way to
go about this process.

00:37:39.320 --> 00:37:42.170
It is be who you are,
be proud of who you are,

00:37:42.170 --> 00:37:43.590
put your best foot forward

00:37:43.590 --> 00:37:45.043
and it does work out.

00:37:46.610 --> 00:37:47.680
- Well Jim, thank you so much

00:37:47.680 --> 00:37:48.597
and I love...

00:37:48.597 --> 00:37:49.890
Every time I talk to you,

00:37:49.890 --> 00:37:51.810
I think you represent authenticity.

00:37:51.810 --> 00:37:53.540
You represent...

00:37:53.540 --> 00:37:54.910
My advice to the students out there

00:37:54.910 --> 00:37:56.050
is be like Jim Nondorf.

00:37:56.050 --> 00:37:58.860
Don't be exactly like
him, but he is himself.

00:37:58.860 --> 00:38:00.500
He's not trying to filter out,

00:38:00.500 --> 00:38:01.570
he has no talking points.

00:38:01.570 --> 00:38:02.880
I've talked to admissions officers

00:38:02.880 --> 00:38:05.570
that are just reading, talking points out

00:38:05.570 --> 00:38:07.460
that their committee has put together

00:38:07.460 --> 00:38:08.390
and that's not you

00:38:08.390 --> 00:38:10.430
and I think the same thing
is true for students.

00:38:10.430 --> 00:38:13.860
If, you have a committee
giving you talking points,

00:38:13.860 --> 00:38:16.313
probably not a good authentic application.

00:38:17.670 --> 00:38:18.503
So, be yourself

00:38:18.503 --> 00:38:20.380
and I gotta say, my own
application in college,

00:38:20.380 --> 00:38:22.050
I didn't know what a good
application looked like.

00:38:22.050 --> 00:38:24.940
So, I drew a picture and
I wrote this crazy dream

00:38:24.940 --> 00:38:28.560
that I had and people thought
I was crazy to submit that

00:38:28.560 --> 00:38:29.929
but it got me in.

00:38:29.929 --> 00:38:30.890
(giggling)

00:38:30.890 --> 00:38:32.330
- Yeah, well, Sal,

00:38:32.330 --> 00:38:34.440
I can't say thank you
enough for all you do.

00:38:34.440 --> 00:38:36.850
I mean, you're a great fan and
I love spending time with you

00:38:36.850 --> 00:38:39.410
and really what you do

00:38:39.410 --> 00:38:41.870
and how you are changing
lives through Khan Academy

00:38:41.870 --> 00:38:42.937
is amazing.

00:38:42.937 --> 00:38:44.530
So, I hope you hear that every day

00:38:44.530 --> 00:38:45.917
'cause I think about it every day.

00:38:45.917 --> 00:38:47.610
So, really thank you.

00:38:47.610 --> 00:38:48.910
- No, Jim and I gotta thank you.

00:38:48.910 --> 00:38:51.660
The love is mutual and
for those who don't know,

00:38:51.660 --> 00:38:53.110
Jim and I were always brainstorming

00:38:53.110 --> 00:38:54.970
ways that Khan Academy
can be even more helpful

00:38:54.970 --> 00:38:57.990
for folks to get in college applications.

00:38:57.990 --> 00:38:59.110
Jim and I were just brainstorming,

00:38:59.110 --> 00:39:01.210
are there ways that students
could take screenshots

00:39:01.210 --> 00:39:03.830
and show it to the college
admissions of the world

00:39:03.830 --> 00:39:05.430
and it sounds like it
could be really valuable.

00:39:05.430 --> 00:39:08.485
So we're excited about
exploring stuff like that.

00:39:08.485 --> 00:39:09.318
- All right, take care.

00:39:09.318 --> 00:39:10.810
Thanks so much.

00:39:10.810 --> 00:39:12.120
- Thanks everyone.

00:39:12.120 --> 00:39:15.780
So, thanks for attending
today's daily live stream.

00:39:15.780 --> 00:39:17.630
I'll remind us, it's
Teacher Appreciation Week.

00:39:17.630 --> 00:39:19.686
So I wanna make sure I do
get that call out that,

00:39:19.686 --> 00:39:21.810
huge appreciation to all the teachers

00:39:21.810 --> 00:39:24.970
and I'll say even admissions
officers in my life

00:39:24.970 --> 00:39:27.220
and other people's lives

00:39:27.220 --> 00:39:28.510
but I hope you all got a lot of it.

00:39:28.510 --> 00:39:29.800
I hope we can bring Jim on again

00:39:29.800 --> 00:39:30.950
'cause I think there's a lot of questions

00:39:30.950 --> 00:39:32.230
that we didn't get to

00:39:32.230 --> 00:39:33.660
but I want everyone to stay safe,

00:39:33.660 --> 00:39:35.800
stay healthy and we will see you tomorrow

00:39:35.800 --> 00:39:36.913
on the live stream.

